WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:05.380
To be forlorn is to despair, to be alone without purpose.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.440
These are the souls drawn into the painting,

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.600
to the cold, gentle world of Ariandel

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:17.100
Some, like Elfrida, they lost faith in their duty

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:21.100
and some, like the followers, simply outlived their duty.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.660
And others, like the Millwood Knights we'll be talking about in this video,

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:29.880
had their duty and their home taken from them.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.780
Millwood is a land of primitive earth worship

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:38.740
and it's fabled knights were know to fight hand in hand with the earth itself.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:43.120
This hammer, for example, with its head of naturally formed stone

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:49.300
and an attack that rips the earth up from under you, is simbolic of that relationship.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.420
In fact, the chieftain knights who we disturb in the painting,

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:57.420
would actually be more accurately called high priests.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:03.460
Many of their heavy bronze weapons, for example, actually have a modest faith requirement

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.400
and they were normally used in sacred rites, not battle.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:13.860
Upon the blades of these weapons, like the battleaxe, is this symbol of the ethereal oak,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.180
a sacred tree, which is clearly at the heart of their civilization.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.800
This tree appears on their weaponry, on their capes, on their shields,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:32.860
and even their helmets are adorned with antlers believed to grow from the blessed beast of the ethereal oak.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.360
Judging from the horns, I think that would be a kind of a stag,

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:38.660
strange, right?

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.860
It's a very curious tree from all descriptions,

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:45.320
and it also appers to be a very bountiful tree,

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.900
considering they cut from it the horns for their helmets

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.020
and maybe even the wood for their shields.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:56.960
I'm very confident that the ethereal oak is actually a spirit tree,

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.660
as they are described in other item descriptions,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.740
for the word ethereal can mean: not of this world

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:10.280
and spirit trees have been referenced multiple times to be otherworldly, the things of dreams.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:16.540
In all the games, spirit trees are actually some of the kindest beings in the series

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:21.440
for with every new spirit tree mentioned, we learn a little bit more about them

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.200
In dark souls 2, for example,

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:29.380
a spirit tree was said to have turned itself into a shield, to protect a small, frightened child

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:35.720
In the sunken kingdom, we found one that repairs the weapons you hit it with

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.880
and, even in the Undead Settlement,

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:45.980
the Cursed Rotted Greatwood consumed the curses of the townsfolk, at its own sacrifice

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:52.440
They're very selfless and noble beings, considering all the sacrifices they go through

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:58.140
and here, to the Milwood knights, is this tree so bountiful,

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.600
that an entire society was built around it

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.980
Unfortunately, of course, according to the Milwood armour,

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:10.260
when the fabled Milwood Forest was discovered, it was a vacant ruin,

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:15.200
the only thing left, was the ethereal oak, stood rotting

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:22.400
It's said that arranged around the rotting ethereal oak, around the bases of their civilization,

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.940
were the belongings of the Millwood knights

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:28.520
and they were arranged neatly on the ground

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:34.460
yet despite the ruin and the rotting tree, no corpses were found

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:39.380
And I believe the reason the description mentions that no corpses were found

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:44.820
is because the knights themselves, their being, not necessarily their belongings,

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.360
were drawn into the painting

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:48.980
but we'll get to that

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.720
So, what caused this?

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:56.500
What, or who, caused their forest to be brought to ruin?

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:00.120
Well, we find an answer in the Millwood Greatbows

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:06.100
which were crafted and wielded by the knights, so that thay could face their sworn enemy

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.880
This sworn enemy was, and I quote, "the Abyss Dragon"

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:17.380
and the closest thing we have to an Abyss Dragon, in my opinion, in Souls lore,

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:19.720
is the Black Dragon Kalameet

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:26.380
which is an ancient dragon that spews abyss tainted flame and is known as the bringer of calamity

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.240
So, for an abyss dragon who brought ruin to the Millwood forest,

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:36.280
I think that Kalameet, the calamitous dragon, certainly fits the bill,

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:37.440
don't you?

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.080
For posterity, I considered some other things,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:44.960
I considered the Puss of Man, because it resembles a dragon,

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:50.180
but there are multiple Puss of Men and they already have a name and it's not 'Abyss Dragon'

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:57.700
There's also Kaathe, a primordial serpent, and serpents are known as imperfect dragons,

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:01.820
and he's an imperfect dragon who resides in the Abyss,

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:04.600
however, he doesn't really fit either.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:08.640
The Millwood knights created Greatbows to take down this creature

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.800
and, you know, it makes a great degree of sense to think

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:18.200
that they would craft greatbows to shoot down giant flying beasts, such as Kalameet

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:25.040
and this exact thing has already happened, because it was Kalameet on the receiving end of a greatarrow

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.240
in another forest, long ago

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.640
So this could be a sort of repeat of that scenario

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.620
in the same sense that so many other scenarios are repeated in Dark Souls 3

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.640
But regardless, right, whoever this dragon was,

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:41.180
even if it isn't Kalameet,

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:46.380
it would seem that their greatbows, they weren't enough to take this thing down

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.040
The Millwood forest was brought to ruin

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.900
and, according to their captain's ashes,

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:57.660
the Millwood knights lost the forest they called home and began a journey so long,

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:05.880
that even thoughts of their sworn enemy washed away, replaced by a vision of a cold and brittle wood

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:11.480
The Knights, forlorn, withdrawn into the Painted World of Ariandel

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:18.720
and they likely found a home to wash away the trauma of their past and the terrible things they've seen

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:24.460
a save place, at least until the unkindled ash comes to kill them all, promising...

